            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-21860",
              "name": "LanguageSpecificPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": " Werkzeug safe_join() allows Windows special device names with compound extensions"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Werkzeug is a comprehensive WSGI web application library. Prior to version 3.1.5, Werkzeug's safe_join function allows path segments with Windows device names that have file extensions or trailing spaces. On Windows, there are special device names such as CON, AUX, etc that are implicitly present and readable in every directory. Windows still accepts them with any file extension, such as CON.txt, or trailing spaces such as CON. This issue has been patched in version 3.1.5."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-21860",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-21860\nSeverity: MEDIUM\nPackage: Werkzeug\nFixed Version: 3.1.5\nLink: [CVE-2026-21860](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-21860)\nWerkzeug is a comprehensive WSGI web application library. Prior to version 3.1.5, Werkzeug's safe_join function allows path segments with Windows device names that have file extensions or trailing spaces. On Windows, there are special device names such as CON, AUX, etc that are implicitly present and readable in every directory. Windows still accepts them with any file extension, such as CON.txt, or trailing spaces such as CON. This issue has been patched in version 3.1.5.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-21860**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|MEDIUM|Werkzeug|3.1.5|[CVE-2026-21860](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-21860)|\n\nWerkzeug is a comprehensive WSGI web application library. Prior to version 3.1.5, Werkzeug's safe_join function allows path segments with Windows device names that have file extensions or trailing spaces. On Windows, there are special device names such as CON, AUX, etc that are implicitly present and readable in every directory. Windows still accepts them with any file extension, such as CON.txt, or trailing spaces such as CON. This issue has been patched in version 3.1.5."
              },
              "properties": {
